1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,874
A 3 nanometer chip doesn't have anything on it that's 3 nanometers.

2
00:00:05,154 --> 00:00:06,689
So what does the name mean?

3
00:00:07,439 --> 00:00:13,812
Decades ago, the number matched a real feature on the chip, roughly the length of a transistor's gate.

4
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:17,079
As chips got smaller, that link broke.

5
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:22,321
Today, the parts of a transistor are many times larger than the number in the name.

6
00:00:23,071 --> 00:00:24,996
Now it's a generation label.

7
00:00:25,276 --> 00:00:28,861
Smaller numbers usually mean denser, more efficient chips.

8
00:00:29,141 --> 00:00:33,087
But one company's 3 nanometer isn't the same as another's.

9
00:00:33,367 --> 00:00:38,637
Intel even renamed one of its processes from 10 nanometer to Intel 7.

10
00:00:39,387 --> 00:00:42,900
So compare real performance, not the number in the name.

11
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,746
More PC hardware explained on the channel.

